### Anything else?

**Suggested fix:** make `FakeAsyncTestZoneSpec` cancel a task only in the instance that scheduled it, and hand a task no instance scheduled to the parent delegate. That is the same thing `ProxyZoneSpec` does when it has no delegate. The owner has to be recorded across instances, not per instance: with two `fakeAsync()` blocks in one spec (e.g. `beforeEach(fakeAsync(...))` and `it(fakeAsync(...))`), a fake timer left by the first and cancelled during the second would otherwise go native with a fake id. Sketch:

```ts
const scheduledBy = new WeakMap<Task, FakeAsyncTestZoneSpec>();   // module-level, shared by all instances

onScheduleTask(delegate, current, target, task) {
  // ...existing switch; wherever task.data['handleId'] is assigned from _setTimeout/_setInterval:
  scheduledBy.set(task, this);
  return task;
}

onCancelTask(delegate, current, target, task) {
  const owner = scheduledBy.get(task);
  if (owner === undefined) {
    return delegate.cancelTask(target, task);   // no fake zone scheduled it: let the real timer be cleared natively
  }
  // ...existing switch, run against owner instead of this
}
```

Changing how fake ids are allocated alone would not be enough. The cancel path would still treat a foreign task's native id as a fake one.

**Workaround:** patch the same two hooks from outside, after `zone.js/testing` has loaded and before any spec runs. The `@angular/build:unit-test` Karma runner ignores `setupFiles`, so import it from a spec module (or from your own test entry point):

```ts
declare const Zone: any;

const fakeAsyncHooks = Zone['FakeAsyncTestZoneSpec'].prototype;
const scheduledBy = new WeakMap<object, object>();
const originalScheduleTask = fakeAsyncHooks.onScheduleTask;
const originalCancelTask = fakeAsyncHooks.onCancelTask;

fakeAsyncHooks.onScheduleTask = function (this: object, delegate: any, current: any, target: any, task: any) {
  const scheduled = originalScheduleTask.call(this, delegate, current, target, task);
  scheduledBy.set(scheduled, this);
  return scheduled;
};

fakeAsyncHooks.onCancelTask = function (delegate: any, current: any, target: any, task: any) {
  const owner = scheduledBy.get(task);
  return owner === undefined
    ? delegate.cancelTask(target, task)
    : originalCancelTask.call(owner, delegate, current, target, task);
};
```

With this in place both specs above pass when run on their own.

### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

forms

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

I maintain a component library and moved one of our inputs to a `FormValueControl` for Angular 22. While testing it, I found a case where the control and its field stop agreeing on the value, and nothing brings them back together.

It happens when the control writes its own `value`, and then, before change detection runs, the field is set back to the value the control last received from it. The field ends up with that value, but the control keeps the one it wrote.

Here's a small example: a text input that clears itself on Escape, inside a form that resets on Escape. Both handlers run for the same keypress. It's a complete `src/main.ts`, so it can be pasted into a new Angular project as is.

```ts
import { Component, model, signal, viewChild } from '@angular/core';
import { FormField, FormValueControl, form } from '@angular/forms/signals';
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-clearable-input',
  template: `<input #input [value]="value()" (input)="value.set(input.value)" (keydown.escape)="value.set('')" />`,
})
export class ClearableInput implements FormValueControl<string> {
  readonly value = model('');
}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [FormField, ClearableInput],
  template: `
    <div (keydown.escape)="nameForm().reset({ name: 'Ada' })">
      <app-clearable-input [formField]="nameForm.name" />
    </div>
    <p>Field value: "{{ nameForm.name().value() }}"</p>
    <p>Control value: "{{ control().value() }}"</p>
  `,
})
export class App {
  protected readonly nameForm = form(signal({ name: 'Ada' }));
  protected readonly control = viewChild.required(ClearableInput);
}

bootstrapApplication(App);
```

Click into the input and press Escape without typing first. The field says "Ada", but the control, and the input on screen, are empty. Pressing Escape again doesn't help. If you type something before pressing Escape, both end up at "Ada" as expected. Setting the field with `nameForm.name().value.set('Ada')` instead of `reset()` goes wrong the same way.

From reading the source, I think the cause is in `customControlCreate` in `packages/forms/signals/src/directive/control_custom.ts`. It only pushes `controlValue` into the control when it differs from the value it pushed last time. When the control writes its own model, the `listenToCustomControlModel` callback updates the field but not that remembered value, so setting the old value again looks like no change and is skipped. The CVA path in `control_cva.ts` already handles this: its `registerOnChange` callback updates the remembered value, which came in with #67866 for #67847. I tried the same change in the custom control path locally (setting `bindings['controlValue'] = value` in that callback) and it fixes the example above.

This looks related to #57124 and #59376, where a `model()` input drifts away from a binding that sets the same value again. There it was described as the contract of a one-way `[x]` binding, but `[formField]` keeps the field and the control in sync in both directions, so here it seems like a bug rather than a design question.

